# Release-age floor, in days. npm resolves a version range only to releases
# published more than this many days ago. A compromised publish is typically
# caught and unpublished within hours, well inside that window.
#
# npm applies the floor wherever it resolves a range: npm install, npm update,
# and every package-lock.json Renovate writes, lock file maintenance included.
# Renovate reads this file, and when it sets min-release-age, Renovate passes
# npm no --before of its own, so this value is the floor on those lockfiles.
# npm ci installs package-lock.json as written and does not re-check the age of
# its entries. npm releases before 11.10.0 do not define the key and apply no
# floor.
#
# Keep this at or below minimumReleaseAge in the github>nanohype/.github
# Renovate preset. Renovate proposes an update once its release is that old,
# and a larger value here makes npm refuse the version Renovate proposes.
#
# Security fixes are held to this floor too. The preset's vulnerabilityAlerts
# block proposes a fix without waiting for it to age, and while the fix is
# younger than this floor, npm refuses it with ETARGET, so Renovate cannot
# write a lockfile for it. To take a younger release on purpose, set its
# version in package.json and run:
#   npm install --min-release-age=0
# The command-line value overrides this file for that one run.
min-release-age=3
